  "findings": [
    "Both groups resolved both tasks and completed normally without failed or duplicate tool calls.",
    "The treatment reduced provider-reported input tokens by 49.89% while preserving resolved rate.",
    "The strongest paired evidence is long_policy_window: both runs fully read all 24 contracts, the treatment used one more model step, yet input tokens fell by 59.69% after five history compactions.",
    "long_route_normalization did not fold history; it only pruned oversized tool results, and also used three fewer model steps, so its 26.56% token reduction is confounded by stochastic trajectory differences.",
    "Token reduction did not translate proportionally to cost reduction; the stored response usage cannot separate cache-hit, cache-miss, input, and output pricing effects.",
    "tool_results_pruned is a cumulative per-request operation count, not a count of unique tool result messages."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "There are only two controlled synthetic tasks and one stochastic run per condition.",
    "The task instruction deliberately requires batched contract inspection to exercise long-context behavior.",
    "One treatment task changed its search trajectory, so aggregate deltas cannot be attributed exclusively to compaction.",
    "The suite does not represent real GitHub Issue or SWE-bench performance.",
    "Local paths, secrets, raw model responses, test stdout, workspaces, and full trajectories are excluded."
  ]
